---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "blessed protein powder",
      "blessed plant protein",
      "ehplabs blessed protein",
      "is blessed protein third party tested",
      "blessed vegan protein review"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Plant-Based Picks",
    link: "/best-plant-based-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Blessed Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      For anyone weighing EHPlabs' Blessed plant protein: what the current labels say, what the testing claim does and does not cover, what a bag costs per 25 g, and the Amazon pea proteins to buy instead if you want a certification or a lower price.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#who-makes-blessed">Who Makes Blessed</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Products</a>
      <a href="#label">What Is in It</a>
      <a href="#third-party-tested">Third-Party Testing</a>
      <a href="#sodium-iron">Sodium and Iron</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#buyer-feedback">Taste and Mixing</a>
      <a href="#vs">Blessed vs Alternatives</a>
      <a href="#who-should-buy">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Blessed protein powder is EHPlabs' sprouted pea protein, and it gives 23 g of protein per scoop for 120 to 150 calories, per the labels at the time of writing. What changes the verdict is testing and sodium: EHPlabs says each batch is lab tested, but we found no NSF or Informed Sport certification, and newer flavors carry 600 mg or more of sodium. Below: label, testing, cost and alternatives.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Blessed Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Blessed is a decent pea protein with a strong flavor range, a clean sweetener choice and a weak testing story. Every current Blessed label gives 23 g of protein per scoop from sprouted pea protein isolate, sweetened with stevia, for about $1.99 per 25 g of protein at EHPlabs' list price. EHPlabs says it tests each batch, but it publishes no results and we found no NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport listing for it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if</strong> you are dairy-free or vegan, want a flavored pea protein that avoids sucralose, and like dessert flavors such as Banana Bread or Cookies &amp; Cream. <strong>Skip it if</strong> you are drug tested in sport, watch your sodium, or mainly want the cheapest plant protein per gram. In those cases the Amazon alternatives below do the job for less, and two of them carry a certification.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Question</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Answer (per label or brand, at the time of writing)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Who makes it?</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">EHPlabs, the company behind OxyShred and OxyWhey</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein per scoop</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">23 g (24 g in Cookies &amp; Cream)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein source</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sprouted pea protein isolate only</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sweetener</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stevia leaf extract (Cookies &amp; Cream adds coconut sugar)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Third-party certified?</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No NSF or Informed Sport listing found; brand says it batch-tests</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cost per 25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.99 (30 serves at $54.95)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">On Amazon?</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not when we checked</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="who-makes-blessed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Who Makes Blessed Protein, and Where Can You Buy It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Blessed Protein is made by EHPlabs, the supplement company best known for the OxyShred fat burner and OxyWhey whey isolate. Blessed sits in EHPlabs' protein menu next to OxyWhey and IsoPept, labelled as its vegan protein, and it is sold through EHPlabs' own storefronts for the US, Australia, the UK, Canada and Singapore. The brand markets it as a pea protein that is smooth and creamy without the usual plant-protein aftertaste.
          </p>

          <p>
            The easiest place to buy it in the US is ehplabs.com. At the time of writing the US store listed the 30-serve tub at $54.95 in three flavors (Vanilla Chai, Banana Bread and Cookies &amp; Cream), plus a 45-serve bag in Strawberry Mylk at $90 and a two-bag Strawberry Mylk deal at $90. Shipping is free on US orders over $100, per EHPlabs, and costs from $6.95 below that, which matters if you only want one tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            We could not find Blessed on Amazon. A search for EHPlabs on Amazon returned OxyWhey listings sold by EHPlabs LLC, but no Blessed listing from the brand. That is why the product cards below pair the two Blessed products with three Amazon pea and plant proteins we could verify. If you see Blessed from a third-party seller, check the seller and the expiry date before buying, since protein sold far from the brand's own channel can be old stock.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more thing to know before you read flavor reviews: Blessed has been reformulated and renamed over the years. EHPlabs' FAQ still describes the original three flavors (Salted Caramel, Vanilla Chai and Choc Coconut), and the product page carries supplement panels for flavors the US store no longer lists, such as S'mores, Cinnamon Churros, Blueberry Pie and Chocolate Mylk. Reviews of a flavor you cannot buy tell you little about the tub you would receive today, so read the current panel for the flavor you pick.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Blessed Protein and the Closest Amazon Alternatives
          </h2>

          <p>
            EHPlabs sells several of its products on Amazon, including OxyWhey, but we could not find Blessed listed there when we searched. The first two cards are Blessed products priced from ehplabs.com. The last three are Amazon pea and plant proteins we could verify, chosen for a certification, a lower price or a flavored, stevia-free alternative. Cost per 25 g uses each label's servings and protein and the price we saw.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is in Blessed Plant Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Blessed is a single-source pea protein: the first ingredient on every current panel is raw sprouted pea protein isolate, and there is no rice, soy or pumpkin protein in the blend. Per the labels on ehplabs.com at the time of writing, a scoop of 32 to 38 g gives 23 g of protein, 130 to 150 calories, 3 to 4 g of fat and 4 to 8 g of carbohydrate, depending on the flavor. Cookies &amp; Cream lists 24 g of protein and 0 g of total sugars in a 35.5 g scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rest of the scoop is there for texture and taste. Most flavors use a sunflower oil creamer (sunflower oil, maltodextrin and, in the newer flavors, modified corn starch and mono- and diglycerides), natural flavor, salt, stevia leaf extract, vegetable gums and psyllium seed powder. Several newer flavors add MCT oil, older ones used inulin fiber, and the colored flavors use beet juice powder or spirulina extract rather than artificial dyes. That creamer is what makes Blessed thicker and smoother than a plain pea isolate, and it is also where most of the fat and carbohydrate comes from.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cookies &amp; Cream is the outlier, and it is one of the three flavors currently sold in the US tub. Its ingredient list includes cocoa powder, MCT oil powder, coconut sugar and carrageenan in its gum blend, and the panel still reads 0 g of added sugar per scoop. If you avoid carrageenan, pick Vanilla Chai or Banana Bread, whose current panels use guar gum, gum acacia and xanthan gum instead. The discontinued Cookie Crunch flavor also used carrageenan.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is Blessed a complete protein?</h3>

          <p>
            EHPlabs says Blessed has a comparable amino acid profile to whey, including all essential amino acids, but it does not publish an amino acid table on the US product page, so we cannot give you a leucine figure. Pea protein is naturally lower in methionine than whey, and a 2019 pilot study the brand itself cites (Banaszek and colleagues) found similar training adaptations from whey and pea protein over eight weeks. For most people eating a varied diet, the amino profile of a pea protein is not the deciding factor.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Per Scoop, Current US Flavors (label)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">23 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">130-150</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Calories</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">0 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Total sugars (where printed)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">5-7 mg</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Iron</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is Blessed Protein Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Blessed is not third-party certified by NSF or Informed Sport as far as we could find. EHPlabs says on its product page that each batch is HPLC, UPLC and LCMS tested to ensure purity of ingredients and the absence of contaminants. Those are laboratory methods, not certifications, and the brand does not say which lab runs the tests or publish batch results.
          </p>

          <p>
            The difference matters for two groups. Athletes who are drug tested need a product listed in a certification database such as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport, because those programs test for banned substances and let you look up the batch. A brand statement that it tests its own product does not give you that protection. We did not find Blessed in either program's results, and a retailer listing we saw explicitly said it is not Informed Sport certified. Our <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/">guide to NSF certified protein powder</a> lists products that are.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second group is anyone worried about heavy metals, which is a fair concern with any plant protein. The Cookies &amp; Cream panel on ehplabs.com carries a California Proposition 65 warning that the product can expose you to lead and/or arsenic. A Prop 65 warning is not a test result, and many plant proteins carry one because the threshold for a warning is low. But EHPlabs does not publish heavy-metal numbers, so there is nothing to compare against. Our page on <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> covers brands that do publish results.
          </p>

          <p>
            What Blessed does have is a manufacturing claim and a halal claim. EHPlabs says Blessed is made on its own dedicated vegan production line, so it does not contact animal or dairy products, and the 45-serve bag says it is produced in a GMP certified facility. The brand's FAQ also answers yes to whether Blessed is halal certified. Those are useful for vegans and halal buyers, but they are not a substitute for an independent contaminant or banned-substance test.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">In-house testing is not certification.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If you are drug tested, buy only a product you can find in the NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport database. Blessed is not one, as far as we could find; NOW Sports Pea Protein, per NOW, is.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="sodium-iron" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Much Sodium and Iron Does Blessed Have?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Newer Blessed flavors are high in sodium for a protein powder: Banana Bread lists 650 mg, Cookies &amp; Cream 640 mg and Cinnamon Churros 600 mg per scoop, per the panels on ehplabs.com at the time of writing. That is about 28% of the daily value in one scoop, before you add milk or anything else. The older flavors listed 330 to 450 mg, and the 45-serve Strawberry Mylk bag lists 400 mg.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pea protein isolate is naturally salty, because sodium is used in the extraction, and many brands add more salt to round out the flavor. Even so, 650 mg is high next to a plain pea isolate such as NOW Sports Pea Protein at 320 mg per scoop, per its label. Two scoops of Banana Bread a day would put you at 1,300 mg of sodium from protein powder alone, more than half of the 2,300 mg upper limit most guidelines use.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have high blood pressure, kidney disease or a doctor-advised sodium limit, that is a reason to choose a lower-sodium flavor or a different product, and to check with your doctor or a registered dietitian before adding any protein powder. Kidney disease in particular can also change how much protein you should eat, so it is worth asking before you start.
          </p>

          <p>
            The upside of the label is iron. Pea protein is a good source, and most Blessed panels list 5 to 7 mg per scoop, about 30 to 38% of the daily value; Cookies &amp; Cream lists 7 mg. That is useful for vegans, vegetarians and menstruating women who struggle to get enough. The 45-serve Strawberry Mylk panel lists only 3 mg, a reminder that the bag and the tub are not quite the same formula. Too much iron is a problem for a small number of people with conditions such as hemochromatosis, who should ask their doctor before using any iron-rich powder.
          </p>
        </div>

