        <div id="buyer-feedback" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What Do Buyers Say About Blessed's Taste and Mixing?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buyers mostly praise Blessed's texture, and their verdicts on taste split by flavor. On the reviews.io pages linked from EHPlabs' stores, a common theme is that Blessed is smooth rather than chalky or gritty, which is the usual complaint about pea protein. Several buyers also say it is easy on the stomach, which fits a dairy-free product. We have not tasted it ourselves; everything here is buyer feedback.
          </p>

          <p>
            Salted Caramel, chocolate flavors and Banana Bread come up most often as favorites in the reviews we read. Vanilla Chai divides people: some buyers like it warmed with plant milk, as EHPlabs suggests, while others call it the flavor they would not reorder. Strawberry Mylk draws both fans and a few comparisons to a medicine taste. Cinnamon Churros and S'mores, neither currently listed in the US store, drew some of the harshest comments.
          </p>

          <p>
            The most common complaints are sweetness and aftertaste. A few buyers say Blessed is too sweet and cut it with unflavored protein, which is a familiar issue with stevia. Others report a lingering aftertaste. One reviewer said the taste did not work in a shake but was fine in protein pancakes, and EHPlabs itself says Blessed can be baked without affecting the protein. If you are unsure about a flavor, the baking route is a reasonable fallback.
          </p>

          <p>
            On mixing, EHPlabs recommends one scoop in 6 to 8 ounces of chilled water or almond milk in a shaker. The gum and creamer blend makes Blessed thicker than a plain isolate, which buyers mostly see as a plus. If you prefer a thin, watery shake, use more liquid. For a broader look at how plant proteins compare on flavor, see our <a href="/best-tasting-protein-powder/">best-tasting protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Blessed vs Naked Pea, OWYN and Other Plant Proteins
          </h2>

          <p>
            Blessed beats plain pea isolates on flavor and loses to them on price, certification and sodium. Against Naked Pea, it costs nearly three times as much per gram and gives 4 g less protein per scoop, but it is ready to drink with water while Naked Pea needs flavoring. Naked shows an NSF Contents Certified badge, per the brand. Our <a href="/naked-nutrition-protein-powder-review/">Naked Nutrition review</a> covers the rest of that range.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against OWYN Pro Elite, the closest flavored rival on Amazon, Blessed is lighter and simpler. OWYN gives 30 g of protein in two scoops for 180 calories, uses monk fruit instead of stevia, and adds amino acids, vitamins and a probiotic. Blessed gives 23 g in one scoop for 130 to 150 calories with a shorter list. OWYN costs about $1.54 per 25 g on Amazon against Blessed's $1.99. The <a href="/owyn-protein-powder-review/">OWYN review</a> has its full label.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against the big organic plant brands, Blessed is a different product. Orgain and Garden of Life use multi-source blends, often with organic certification, while Blessed uses pea alone and is not certified organic. If organic matters to you, read our <a href="/orgain-protein-powder-review/">Orgain review</a> or the <a href="/garden-of-life-protein-powder-review/">Garden of Life review</a>. If you are weighing plant protein against dairy at all, <a href="/whey-vs-plant-protein/">whey vs plant protein</a> lays out the trade-offs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once Blessed is in our label database, the programmatic <a href="/compare/">compare pages</a> will put it side by side with every other plant protein on protein, sodium, sweetener and cost per 25 g. For a pea-only shortlist, our <a href="/best-pea-protein-powder/">best pea protein powder</a> guide ranks the field, and the <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder</a> pillar covers blends as well. Shoppers comparing women-focused brands may also want our <a href="/glo-protein-powder-review/">Glo protein powder review</a>, published alongside this one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Buy Blessed Protein, and Who Should Skip It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Blessed is a good fit for vegans and dairy-free buyers who want a flavored shake they can mix with water and who prefer stevia to sucralose. The single-source pea protein, the dedicated vegan production line EHPlabs describes, the halal claim and the iron content all suit that buyer. If you already use EHPlabs products and like the brand's flavors, Blessed is the obvious plant option in its range.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you compete in a drug-tested sport. Without an NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport listing, you have no batch-level assurance against banned substances, and an in-house testing claim does not change that. NOW Sports Pea Protein, which NOW lists as Informed Sport certified, is the cheaper and safer choice for you.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip or choose carefully if you watch sodium. At 600 to 650 mg per scoop in the newer flavors, Blessed is saltier than most protein powders. The Strawberry Mylk bag, at 400 mg, is the gentler option within the range. Anyone with high blood pressure, kidney disease, a food allergy or who is pregnant should talk to a doctor or registered dietitian before adding a protein powder; EHPlabs' own FAQ also tells anyone under a practitioner's care to check first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if price per gram is what matters most. At about $1.99 per 25 g at list price, Blessed costs two to three times as much as plain pea isolates, and you can get a flavored plant protein on Amazon for less. The exception is the two-bag Strawberry Mylk deal at about $1.09 per 25 g, which is competitive if you like that flavor.
          </p>
        </div>

