          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading in-house testing as certification</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                EHPlabs says each Blessed batch is HPLC, UPLC and LCMS tested, but that is the brand's own claim with no published results. It is not NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport. Drug-tested athletes should not treat it as equivalent, and should buy only products they can find in a certification database.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the sodium line</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Banana Bread lists 650 mg of sodium per scoop and Cookies &amp; Cream 640 mg, per the labels at the time of writing. Two scoops a day adds more than half of a 2,300 mg daily limit. If you are watching blood pressure, compare panels by flavor and consider the 400 mg Strawberry Mylk bag instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the 45-serve bag at full price</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The bigger bag sounds like the value option, but at $90 for 45 scoops it costs about $2.17 per 25 g, more than the $1.99 of the 30-serve tub. It only makes sense on the two-bag deal, where 90 scoops for $90 brings it down to about $1.09.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting reviews of discontinued flavors</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Much of what you read about Blessed concerns S'mores, Cinnamon Churros, Choc Coconut or Salted Caramel, none of which the US store listed when we checked. Formulas have changed too. Read reviews for the exact flavor you plan to buy, and check its current panel for sodium and gums.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every flavor is carrageenan-free</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most current Blessed flavors use guar gum, gum acacia and xanthan gum, but Cookies &amp; Cream lists carrageenan and cellulose gum, plus coconut sugar. If you avoid carrageenan for digestive reasons, choose Vanilla Chai or Banana Bread, and read the ingredient list again whenever EHPlabs updates the label.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

